+## DEC-0054 — First runtime voluntary Movement is a Monster-only split-movement Combat slice with persisted turn expenditure
+
+- **Date:** 2026-10-03
+- **Status:** Accepted
+- **Context:** TSK-0022 already provides the first authoritative writer of `CombatState.positions`, and TSK-0039 now provides an authoritative packaged Monster base walking speed (`MonsterDefinition.walking_speed`, Goblin 30 ft). The remaining concrete gap is runtime voluntary repositioning. A universal Character/Creature Movement design would still require an unresolved Character speed source and would prematurely pull in Dash, Disengage, Reactions, Opportunity Attacks, terrain, collision, pathfinding, movement modes, and generic resource/geometry abstractions. The first runtime consumer therefore needs to be narrow enough to use only authoritative data that already exist while still supporting the D&D requirement that movement may be split around an Action.
+- **Decision:** Define Architecture §3.38 as the first runtime voluntary-Movement contract. `MoveCombatantCommand` has payload `(combat_id, x, y)` and `actor_id` is the mover; no separate subject id, speed, distance, remaining-capacity, path, mode, or legality field is caller supplied. The Command is Combat-only and active-turn-only. Character actors are rejected in this slice before Monster Definition lookup because no authoritative Character speed exists. A non-Character actor must resolve through the existing `DefinitionSource` as `MonsterDefinition`, have `current_hp > 0`, have `walking_speed is not None`, and already have a `CombatPosition`. `walking_speed == 0` remains valid but permits no positive segment. One Command is one straight segment in the existing integer-feet coordinate system. Its project-defined first-consumer cost is deterministic whole-foot `ceil(sqrt(dx² + dy²))`, implemented with integer arithmetic (`isqrt`) rather than float math; this is a local Movement rule, not a universal geometry service or a reinterpretation of §3.30 reach. A destination equal to the current position is `INVALID_COMMAND` and emits no Event. Add `CombatState.movement_spent: int = 0` after `action_spent`, owned by the existing Combat State Owner and meaning cumulative voluntary Movement already spent by the current active combatant this turn. The field is exact non-negative `int`, has no intrinsic upper bound, is not itself validated against a Definition, and is preferred over persisted remaining capacity. Multiple Move Commands may succeed in one turn; pure resolution derives `new_movement_spent = previous movement_spent + segment distance`, and Application accepts the segment only when that total is `<= walking_speed`. No separate `INVALID_STATE` failure exists merely because previously recorded expenditure exceeds a later-read speed; a positive new segment simply fails the cumulative capacity check with `OUT_OF_RANGE`. Movement is independent of `action_spent`: it neither spends nor is gated by the baseline Action. Add `MoveCombatantResult(combat_id, from_x, from_y, to_x, to_y, distance, movement_spent)` and `CombatantMoved` V1 with payload exactly `combatId/fromX/fromY/toX/toY/distance`, `actorId` equal to the mover, and `causedBy = null`; cumulative `movementSpent` is not duplicated into the Event because the concrete applier derives it from pre-event Combat State plus Event distance. `apply_combatant_moved_v1` validates exact type/version/payload, combat/active-actor correlation, stale source position, non-zero segment, and canonical distance; it replaces the actor position at the same tuple index and increments `movement_spent`, preserving all unrelated Combat facts. `CombatStarted` V1 stays unchanged and obtains `movement_spent=0` from the State default. `TurnAdvanced` V1 stays unchanged and its applier resets both `action_spent=False` and `movement_spent=0`; same-turn `TurnActionSpent`, placement, and Attack persistence preserve Movement expenditure. One successful Move builds one replacement Combat State containing position and expenditure together and calls `StateStore.save()` exactly once. Define planned State schema V10 in §12.13 as strictly additive over V9: only non-null Combat gains required exact integer `movementSpent`; V10 retains every V9 Character lifecycle, Creature Condition, Inventory/Equipment, position, and `actionSpent` semantic, while V5–V9 active Combat decodes `movement_spent=0`. Historical wire shapes remain exact. TSK-0040 performs the production implementation; until then the current writer remains V9. Reactions and Opportunity Attacks are not adjudicated by this slice; their absence is not a rule that Movement never provokes them.
+- **Consequences:** TSK-0040 can be an execution-ready Monster-only vertical slice depending on completed TSK-0022 and TSK-0039, without inventing Character speed or a generic Movement framework. Broad Roadmap `Movement` remains unchecked after this decision and remains unchecked after the narrow implementation because Character Movement, Dash/Disengage, forced movement, terrain/pathfinding/collision/occupancy/elevation, movement modifiers/modes, Reactions, and Opportunity Attacks remain outside scope. TSK-0023 remains separate draft future work and is not a dependency. No production Python, State writer, Event producer, or gameplay behavior changes merely by publishing this decision.
